WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.392
Model provider, system provider, deployer: who you are.

2
00:00:03.672 --> 00:00:07.981
Most confusion about the AI Act comes from not knowing which role you play.

3
00:00:08.261 --> 00:00:21.253
If you train a general-purpose model and make it available in the EU, you are a GPAI model provider and the Article 53 duties (and Article 55, above the systemic-risk threshold) are yours.

4
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:31.283
If you build a product on someone else’s model (an API or open weights) and put it on the EU market under your name, you are the provider of an AI system.

5
00:00:31.563 --> 00:00:38.283
The model duties stay with the model provider; you are responsible for your system’s classification (is it high-risk?)

6
00:00:38.563 --> 00:00:41.507
, its transparency duties, and its documentation.

7
00:00:41.787 --> 00:00:52.496
If you use an AI system in your own operations, you are a deployer, with duties that depend on the risk class (for high-risk uses: human oversight, monitoring, logs).

8
00:00:52.776 --> 00:01:04.680
If you significantly modify a general-purpose model, for example by substantial fine-tuning, the Commission’s guidelines say you may become the provider of the modified model, for the modification.

9
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.413
Minor modifications do not trigger this.

10
00:01:07.693 --> 00:01:24.568
For a typical team building on Claude, GPT, Gemini, or Grok through an API, the practical duties are: tell people when they are talking to an AI (Article 50(1)); make sure generated synthetic content is marked where the rules require it;

11
00:01:24.848 --> 00:01:42.533
check every use case against the high-risk list (screening job applicants or scoring creditworthiness puts you in a very different regime from summarizing meeting notes); support staff AI literacy; and collect the documentation your model provider must give downstream providers,

12
00:01:42.813 --> 00:01:44.520
because you will need it for your own.
